the twelve boys and their soccer coach  who spent more than two weeks trapped in  a cave in Thailand are expected to go  home this week newly released photos  show the boys in the hospital this  morning they can be seen hugging the  Thai Navy medic who took care of them in  the cave and a Warner is in Chiang Rai  Thailand with the latest on their  conditions and a good morning good  morning health officials say the boys  are doing well here some who lost as  much as 11 pounds are starting to gain  that weight back meanwhile their parents  were back at the cave today for a  ceremony volunteers along with the  families gathered at the cave in  Thailand Monday where the 12 boys and  their soccer coach were rescued after  being trapped for more than two weeks  the cave has been closed ever since the  last boy was pulled out there are plans  to make it into an interactive museum  but for now everyone is just grateful to  pay their respects a memorial ceremony  was also held for the former Thai Navy  SEAL who died during the rescue mission  new photos show the 12 Thai boys paying  tribute to the seal from their hospital  room the boys bowed their heads as they  surrounded a drawing of the diver some  later wiped away tears earlier the  government released these video clips of  the boys they said they were feeling  well and thanked their rescuers Thai  officials hope the entire group will go  home by Thursday  well the parents actually went into the  cave for the first time this afternoon  since the boys have been rescued one  father told us it was spooky for him  because he had been preparing himself  for the idea that his son might not  emerge from that cave alive Gail  all right Anna Werner thank you very  much would you guys want to go in the  cave I think I would I think I would  want to say well to understand what the  experience they had been they were  really now that no okay not you so watch  this far with the exception of the death  of that see oh I'm so glad they're okay  thank you Anna
 

